* Idioms whose layout is the algorithm.
*
* Everything in `idioms.bfm` takes the cells it works on as parameters, and
* the expander emits the movement between them. That is the whole point of
* naming cells -- and it is also why those idioms cost what they cost: a value
* is copied when it could have been moved, and a cell is asked whether it is
* zero by spending its value to find out.
*
* The fast algorithms in the catalogue at
* https://esolangs.org/wiki/Brainfuck_algorithms are not written that way.
* They are written as a pointer walking a fixed workspace, and their layout
* notes -- "x, temp0, temp1, y" -- *are* the algorithm. Nothing is copied and
* no cell is named, which is exactly why they are quick, and exactly why `@to`
* cannot express them.
*
* So they are not expressed. They are pasted in, and the two things the
* expander needs are supplied around them:
*
*   - the cells are pinned with `at`, because a snippet made of `>` and `<`
*     needs its neighbours to be its neighbours;
*   - `@here` says where the snippet leaves the cursor, because a `[>+>>]` is
*     a scan and the expander cannot follow one.
*
* **A `@divmod` may appear inside a loop**, which it may not always have been
* able to. A snippet like this one makes the movement a loop emits stop
* meaning anything, and the expander measures a loop's balance in movement --
* so every loop around one used to be a loop it could no longer promise
* anything about, and a `@to` inside it was refused. Since a division belongs
* in a loop, that was most of the use. It now measures a body by where it
* began and where it ended whenever both are known, which is exactly what the
* `@here` below restores. `programs/macros/primes.bfm` is the loop.
*
* The workspace is pinned low, and a program including this file gets its own
* cells above it, so the copies in and out are short. **Cells 0 to 19 belong to
* this file, and nothing will tell you if you take one.** A cell chosen by the
* expander is never one that was pinned, so `@var mine` is safe; two `at`s
* naming the same cell are allowed on purpose, so `@var mine at 6` silently
* becomes another name for `fast_n` and every division after it returns
* nonsense. Pin nothing below 20 in a program that includes this file.
*
* Six of those cells are in front of the workspace and hold nothing. A
* snippet that walks a pointer walks it both ways: this one steps three cells
* to the left of where it started, transiently, and dividing by one is what
* found that out -- as `Attempted to access cell -3`, which is at least the
* kind of failure that cannot be missed.

@include "idioms.bfm"

* Room to the left. Reserved rather than merely left empty, so that a
* program's own cells are given out above them instead of underneath the
* snippet.
@var fast_room0 at 0
@var fast_room1 at 1
@var fast_room2 at 2
@var fast_room3 at 3
@var fast_room4 at 4
@var fast_room5 at 5

@var fast_n at 6
@var fast_gap at 7
@var fast_d at 8
@var fast_rem at 9
@var fast_quo at 10

* Room to the right, for the same reason.
@var fast_room6 at 11
@var fast_room7 at 12

@var fast_flag at 13
@var fast_other at 14
@var fast_temp at 15
@var fast_spare at 16
@var fast_safe at 17
@var fast_one at 18
@var fast_none at 19

* `quotient` and `remainder` are filled in from `n` divided by `d`. Neither `n`
* nor `d` is disturbed.
*
* The snippet is `[->+>-[>+>>]>[+[-<+>]>+>>]<<<<<<]`, verbatim. Its contract is
* not written down anywhere in a form that can be trusted, so it was
* established by running it: starting on a cell holding `n`, with a zero and
* then `d` after it, it finishes on that same cell having left
*
*     0   n   d-remainder   remainder   quotient
*
* across the five cells from where it started. Everything below is moving
* values into that shape and taking two of them back out.
*
* Cells only, so `n` and `d` are at most 255. Wider division is not this.
*
* **Two divisors the snippet cannot do, and the macro can.** Given one it
* walks off the left of its workspace and finishes two cells from where it
* said it would, which makes the `@here` above a lie and everything after it
* wrong -- silently, since `@here` is trusted rather than checked. Given zero
* it has no answer to give. Both are answered here instead, before the snippet
* is reached: one goes into anything exactly as many times as the thing is,
* and nothing goes into anything at all.
*
* Neither hole is written down on the wiki. Both turned up the first time the
* contract was tested against every divisor rather than a convenient one.
@macro divmod(n, d, quotient, remainder) {
    * A divisor the snippet survives. The two it does not are put right
    * afterwards; what matters here is that it is never handed one of them,
    * because what it does with them is not an error but a wrong answer.
    @clear(fast_safe)
    @copy(d, fast_safe, fast_temp)

    * Both awkward divisors are recognised once and remembered, because each
    * is wanted twice: here, to keep the snippet away from them, and below, to
    * put its answer right.
    @is_zero(d, fast_none, fast_temp, fast_spare)
    @put(fast_one, 1)
    @equal(fast_one, d, fast_temp, fast_spare)

    @clear(fast_flag)
    @copy(fast_none, fast_flag, fast_spare)
    @copy(fast_one, fast_flag, fast_spare)
    @to fast_flag
    [
        @put(fast_safe, 2)
        @clear(fast_flag)
    ]

    @clear(fast_n)
    @clear(fast_gap)
    @clear(fast_d)
    @clear(fast_rem)
    @clear(fast_quo)
    @copy(n, fast_n, fast_temp)
    @move(fast_safe, fast_d)

    * The snippet, at the top of the body and not inside anything. The
    * expander measures a loop's balance in movement, and it cannot measure
    * this one's -- so a `[` anywhere around it is a loop it can no longer
    * promise anything about, and it says so.
    @to fast_n
    [->+>-[>+>>]>[+[-<+>]>+>>]<<<<<<]
    @here fast_n

    @clear(quotient)
    @move(fast_quo, quotient)
    @clear(remainder)
    @move(fast_rem, remainder)

    * Nothing goes into anything: no times, with all of it left over.
    @to fast_none
    [
        @clear(quotient)
        @clear(remainder)
        @copy(n, remainder, fast_spare)
        @clear(fast_none)
    ]

    * One goes into anything exactly as many times as the thing is.
    @to fast_one
    [
        @clear(quotient)
        @copy(n, quotient, fast_spare)
        @clear(remainder)
        @clear(fast_one)
    ]
}
